    "abstract": "<p>This chapter appeals to the work of Louise Bourgeois for a model of construction that can incorporate multiple, material processes in the creation of form. Bourgeois’ art foregrounds the role of material as a condition of possibility but also a constraint on the creation, and maintenance of, new forms by the artist. This chapter explores the relationship between the creation of form and material processes in her work, yielding insights into this relationship that can be applied to our understanding of the relationship between selves and bodily processes. It further discusses the nature of the materiality of self-construction in terms of corporeality, using works by Bourgeois which deepen our awareness and understanding of corporeality. Her work both demonstrates the eliminability of the body and testifies to the implications of the cultural context in which it is expressed and interpreted.  In addition to drawing on her work to elaborate a model of corporeal self-construction, the chapter also considers how Bourgeois connected her artworks to her own self-construction.</p>",
    "DOI": "10.3366/edinburgh/9780748693672.003.0004",
