    "abstract": "<p>This chapter addresses the question of how artworks can be employed to develop new identities and new ways of relating to our identity which are not constraining. This chapter argues that re-appropriation of imagery that has been used to oppress us is insufficient. Rather we need to go beyond the normative assumption that identity can be reduced to image or narrative and instead harness bodily processes that cannot be codified in these ways. Discussing artworks by Claude Cahun, Yvonne Rainer, Steve McQueen, ORLAN, Rebecca Horn, Carolee Schneemann, Marina Abramović, Mona Hatoum and Hermann Nitsch  this chapter looks at how artists have explored and engaged the body beyond culture, worked to break habits, cultivate playfulness, and engage drives and other bodily processes. Experimental engagements with the body can provide us with the means to experiment with new modes of selfhood. What emerges from these discussions is that artworks and working on the self are continually in interaction, and that critique and experimentation in one sphere create new possibilities in the other.</p>",
    "DOI": "10.3366/edinburgh/9780748693672.003.0006",
